@@ -0,0 +1,26 @@
+# 0001 — Reservations expire lazily, from the injected clock
+
+Date: 2026-10-29 · Status: accepted
+
+## Context
+
+Holds that nobody confirms must lapse after a TTL (20 minutes by default). The
+package has no I/O and no scheduler, and all time must come from the injected
+`Clock`.
+
+## Decision
+
+There is no sweep job. A reservation is lapsed when `clock.now() >= expires_at`.
+Every read or use of reservations (`get_reservation`, `confirm_reservation`,
+`release_reservation`, `reserve` idempotent replays, `held`, `available`)
+first moves lapsed ACTIVE reservations to EXPIRED and saves them. The cutoff is
+inclusive: at exactly `expires_at` the hold is already gone.
+
+## Consequences
+
+- Expiry needs no caller cooperation and is reproducible under `FixedClock`.
+- The stored status can be stale until something looks at it; code that reads
+  reservations from the repository directly must go through the service's
+  refresh, not trust `status` raw.
+- A database-backed repository later must keep the same rule (e.g. compute
+  `expires_at > now` in queries) rather than add a cron.
